The function `apply_patch` will take two arguments: `text`, which is the original string, and `replacements`, which is a list of tuples where each tuple contains two strings, the first being the substring to be replaced (`old`) and the second being the replacement substring (`new`). The function will iterate over the list of replacements and apply each one to the `text` string in sequence. If any `old` string from the replacements list is not found in the `text`, a `KeyError` will be raised with an appropriate message. This ensures that all specified replacements are made and that the absence of any required substring is clearly communicated. Edge cases include when the `replacements` list is empty, in which case the original `text` is returned unchanged, and when multiple replacements involve overlapping substrings, in which case the replacements are applied strictly from left to right, potentially affecting subsequent replacements.

