The function `parseQuery(qs)` will take a query string as input and convert it into an object where each key-value pair is represented as a property of the object. If a key appears multiple times in the query string, its values will be stored in an array. The function will also handle percent-encoded characters by decoding them to their original form. Edge cases include empty query strings, query strings with only one key-value pair, and query strings with repeated keys but different values. Additionally, the function should correctly parse keys and values that contain special characters or spaces.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function parseQuery(qs) {
  const result = {};
  if (!qs) return result;
  const pairs = qs.split('&');
  for (const pair of pairs) {
    const [key, value] = pair.split('=');
    const decodedKey = decodeURIComponent(key);
    const decodedValue = decodeURIComponent(value);
    if (result[decodedKey]) {
      if (Array.isArray(result[decodedKey])) {
        result[decodedKey].push(decodedValue);
      } else {
        result[decodedKey] = [result[decodedKey], decodedValue];
      }
    } else {
      result[decodedKey] = decodedValue;
    }
  }
  return result;
}

module.exports = parseQuery;
```

