The function `findIndexBy` will take an array of elements of generic type `T` and a predicate function `(t:T) => boolean` as arguments. The predicate function will be used to test each element in the array until it finds one that satisfies the condition, at which point it will return the index of that element. If no element satisfies the predicate, the function will return -1. This implementation ensures that the function works correctly with arrays of any type and handles cases where the predicate might not match any element in the array.

ANSWER:
```typescript
export function findIndexBy<T>(arr: T[], pred: (t: T) => boolean): number {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (pred(arr[i])) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```

