To implement infinite scrolling in a React Native FlatList, you need to handle the `onEndReached` event, which is triggered when the user reaches the end of the list. It's important to set an appropriate `onEndReachedThreshold` to ensure that the event is fired before the user actually reaches the very end, allowing for a smoother loading experience. Additionally, you should manage the state to load more data and update the list accordingly to prevent duplicate items from being displayed.

ANSWER:

```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { FlatList, Text, View } from 'react-native';

const InfiniteScrollFlatList = () => {
  const [data, setData] = useState([]);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const fetchData = async () => {
      setLoading(true);
      // Simulate fetching data from an API
      const newData = await new Promise((resolve) =>
        setTimeout(() => resolve(Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => `Item ${i + (page - 1) * 10}`)), 1000)
      );
      setData((prevData) => [...prevData, ...newData]);
      setLoading(false);
    };

    fetchData();
  }, [page]);

  const renderItem = ({ item }) => (
    <View style={{ padding: 10 }}>
      <Text>{item}</Text>
    </View>
  );

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={data}
      renderItem={renderItem}
      keyExtractor={(item, index) => `${index}`}
      onEndReached={() => {
        if (!loading) {
          setPage((prevPage) => prevPage + 1);
        }
      }}
      onEndReachedThreshold={0.5}
    />
  );
};

export default InfiniteScrollFlatList;
```

