To create a React Native class component that acts as an error boundary, I will implement the `componentDidCatch` lifecycle method to catch errors in the component tree below it. Additionally, I will define a `fallbackRender` method to display a user-friendly message or UI when an error occurs. This approach ensures that errors do not crash the entire app and provide a better user experience. Edge cases include handling different types of errors and ensuring that the fallback UI is accessible and does not cause additional errors itself.

ANSWER:

```javascript
import React, { Component } from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

class ErrorBoundary extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {
    console.error('Error caught by boundary:', error, errorInfo);
  }

  fallbackRender() {
    return (
      <View style={styles.fallbackContainer}>
        <Text style={styles.fallbackText}>An error occurred. Please try again later.</Text>
      </View>
    );
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return this.fallbackRender();
    }

    return this.props.children;
  }
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  fallbackContainer: {
    flex: 1,
    justifyContent: 'center',
    alignItems: 'center',
    backgroundColor: '#f8f8f8',
  },
  fallbackText: {
    fontSize: 18,
    color: '#333',
  },
});

export default ErrorBoundary;
```

